d shadows. Both types of light have their place. The trick is knowing which to use.\\n\\nUnderstanding the difference helps you determine the mood for the photo. Soft light flatters faces and can feel more romantic. Hard light adds edge and drama. If you need to switch between the two, you can soften a hard light by simply adding shade.\"},{\"type\":\"callout\",\"title\":\"Expert advice\",\"text\":\"To use each type of light, adjust your distance and direction. Step back for softness. Move closer for hardness.\"},{\"type\":\"divider\"},{\"type\":\"h2\",\"text\":\"10. Use time of day to your advantage\"},{\"type\":\"p\",\"text\":\"Best for: Portraits, everyday moments\\n\\nThe time of day can change everything. Morning and evening light feel warm and calm, while midday light feels much brighter and energetic. Night brings glow and contrast. You can use each one to your advantage to tell a different story.\\n\\nPlanning a shoot around light saves time and edits later. Golden hour works beautifully for landscapes and candid shots. For nighttime photography, you can work with the glistening of streetlights, car headlights, windows, and more.\"},{\"type\":\"callout\",\"title\":\"Expert advice\",\"text\":\"Try planning your shoot around the time of day that best fits the mood you want to capture. For example, consider the morning or golden hour for quiet moments, and midday light for busy scenes.\"},{\"type\":\"divider\"},{\"type\":\"h2\",\"text\":\"11. Position your subject with the lighting\"},{\"type\":\"p\",\"text\":\"Best for: Portraits\\n\\nWhere your subject stands matters just as much as the light itself. Facing the light helps brighten features. Standing slightly to the side can add depth. Backlighting creates glow and outlines.\\n\\nGood positioning keeps the focus where you want it. This is why you\'ll want to move your subject before you touch your settings. A small shift can change shadows, highlight expressions, and so much more.\"},{\"type\":\"callout\",\"title\":\"Expert advice\",\"text\":\"Start with your subject facing the light source, then rotate slowly until the illumination matches the mood you\'re going for.\"},{\"type\":\"divider\"},{\"type\":\"h2\",\"text\":\"12. Keep an eye on your background, too\"},{\"type\":\"p\",\"text\":\"Best for: Product photos, headshots, landscape photos\\n\\nNotice how lighting doesn\'t stop at your subject. It also spills into the background, helping shape the whole frame. Bright spots can pull attention, while dark corners can add focus or distraction.\\n\\nCheck what\'s behind your subject before shooting. Adjust your angle so that the light supports the scene. This will help your photos feel clean and intentional when you print them later.\"},{\"type\":\"callout\",\"title\":\"Expert advice\",\"text\":\"Watch for backlights in the background. Bright lights or windows can steal focus fast.\"},{\"type\":\"divider\"},{\"type\":\"h2\",\"text\":\"13. Adjust light contrast while editing\"},{\"type\":\"p\",\"text\":\"Best for: Editorial photography, professional portraits\\n\\nThink of editing as fine-tuning the light you captured. Adjusting contrast helps add detail, deepen shadows, and balance highlights without drastically changing the shot. It\'s the final touch that photos need to look their best.\\n\\nA small edit goes a long way. When you\'re editing photos, you\'re aiming for clarity, not perfection. Subtle tweaks help your shots look natural in print and ready to share.\\n\\nIf you’re disappointed in the lighting you’ve captured, don’t worry. It’s possible to fix bad lighting in photos, and you can follow along with expert tutorials to make the changes you need.\"},{\"type\":\"callout\",\"title\":\"Expert advice\",\"text\":\"Less is more when it comes to contrast. It’s easier to build up the effect in small increments than to fix an over-processed image that looks unnatural. Most importantly, always save a duplicate of your original photo before you start editing—this ensures